  "Number": 17,
  "Question": "If \\(y=a+\\dfrac bx,\\) where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are constants, and if \\(y=1\\) when \\(x=-1,\\) and \\(y=5\\) when \\(x=-5,\\) then \\(a+b\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-1\\)",
    "B": "\\(0\\)",
    "C": "\\(1\\)",
    "D": "\\(10\\)",
    "E": "\\(11\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The same constants must satisfy both given input-output pairs. Both \\(x\\)-values are nonzero, so substitution into \\(y=a+b/x\\) gives\n\\[\na-b=1,\\qquad a-\\frac b5=5.\n\\]\nThe coefficients of \\(a\\) are equal, so subtracting the first equation from the second eliminates \\(a\\):\n\\[\n\\left(a-\\frac b5\\right)-(a-b)=5-1,\n\\qquad \\frac45b=4.\n\\]\nThus \\(b=5\\). Substituting into \\(a-b=1\\) gives \\(a=6\\), and therefore\n\\[\na+b=6+5=\\boxed{11},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\text{E}\\).",
